---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { Content as Methodology } from "../../../docs/methodology.md";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<Base title="Methodology">
  <div class="prose">
    <Methodology />
    <h2 id="evidence-tiers-ui">What the badges on this site mean</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Badge</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Credit</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="tierbox">T1</span></td><td>Registry-matched: found on the issuing registry (FedRAMP Marketplace, CSA STAR, DPF list, IAF CertSearch)</td><td>1.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="tierbox">T2</span></td><td>Marketplace-listed: a cloud marketplace listing states it (e.g. AWS "HIPAA eligible")</td><td>0.8</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="tierbox">T3</span></td><td>Document-evidenced: the maintainer reviewed a certificate, letter or report</td><td>0.7</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="tierbox">T4</span></td><td>Vendor-claimed: the framework is named on the vendor's own trust or security page</td><td>0.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="tierbox">T5</span></td><td>Unknown: nothing found in the sources checked</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="tierbox pending">T1</span></td><td>Dashed: evidence found but not credited, because the publisher is not yet confirmed (identity worse than tier 2); the dossier says so once above its ledger</td><td>0</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The trust strip on every result shows five facts, and only one of them is a T-scale: <strong>Publisher</strong> (confirmed, likely, unconfirmed or unknown — who publishes the agent),
      <strong>Evidence</strong> (best active compliance record about the agent itself, T1–T5), <strong>Security</strong> (an NVD keyword search was run for the score; the dossier shows the CVEs the <a href={`${base}/cves/`}>CVE registry</a> links to the agent, never a count of ours),
      <strong>Protocols</strong> (A2A / MCP / ARD observed) and <strong>Issues</strong> (open incidents, CVEs or
      regulator actions). "–" means not checked in this build.
    </p>

    <h2 id="traces">How traces and the incident registry are collected</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>trace</strong> is evidence of what an agent did under attack, in defence or in an incident. It has its own
      five-step scale, kept apart from compliance tiers: T1 a reproducible run with public artifacts · T2 a third-party
      report with a transcript · T3 a vendor report · T4 a vendor claim · T5 none found. "None found" is never a finding
      against the agent — it means nobody has published what the agent does under attack, and the dossier says so.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The incident registry</strong> (<a href={`${base}/traces/`}>/traces/</a>) — one row per real, sourced event
        where an AI agent attacked, defended or was the target, curated from post-mortems, advisories, researcher
        disclosures and the press. Every row is "as reported": figures are the sources' own, MITRE ATT&amp;CK tactics are the
        reviewer's reading, and each row says who reviewed it — a person, or the weekly machine review (marked
        <em>machine-reviewed</em>). Each has a timeline, lessons for defenders, and STIX 2.1 / JSON export.</li>
      <li><strong>The weekly scout</strong> — the Monday refresh reads a curated list of public feeds (press, researcher
        labs, vendor advisories) and keeps any item that names both an agent and an attack as a <em>candidate</em>: title,
        link, date, outlet, snippet. A candidate is a lead, never an incident.</li>
      <li><strong>The weekly machine review — publish by default.</strong> The same run fetches each candidate's article
        and asks a model one structured question against that text only: is this a specific, real event in which an AI
        agent attacked, defended or was the target? The reply (date, role, category, an "as reported" summary, the two
        sides as the source describes them, tactics, models and agents named, a timeline) is validated field by field; a
        field the article does not state stays empty. It is published when the verdict is an incident with confidence of
        at least 0.8 from a source that is not an aggregator; otherwise it is dismissed with the reason kept on the
        candidate. The model is told never to invent a fact, never to reproduce prompts, payloads or commands, and never to
        name a private individual. Every decision is listed in the refresh's pull request, which merges itself once the
        gates pass; a wrong row can be retracted by hand. Nobody waits on a person, and no row is "pending".</li>
      <li><strong>The weekly re-check</strong> — every published incident's source links are re-fetched and the result
        recorded (answers · moved · gone · bot-walled). A link that stops answering is flagged on the incident page; the
        record itself is never changed by the check.</li>
      <li><strong>Per-agent traces</strong> on each dossier — curated write-ups, the registry's own endpoint probes
        (every refresh records each publisher's ARD/A2A/MCP exchange), and vendor-published bundles declared in an ARD
        manifest and hash-checked. All live in an append-only, hash-chained store that CI verifies.</li>
      <li><strong>The harness</strong> — runs an agent against the AgentDojo prompt-injection benchmark and records a
        tier-1 bundle. It is built and tested; it produces public traces only when a model-provider key is configured,
        and until then no public harness trace exists.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="small muted">
      The full rules, the review bar and the schema are in
      <a href="https://github.com/Sushegaad/AgentDossier/blob/main/docs/incidents.md" rel="noopener">docs/incidents.md</a> and
      <a href="https://github.com/Sushegaad/AgentDossier/blob/main/docs/traces.md" rel="noopener">docs/traces.md</a>.
      Behaviour is published, never weapons: scenario inputs and the agent's actions, no payloads against third-party systems.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cves">How the CVE registry is compiled</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={`${base}/cves/`}>CVE list</a> is every record on the official CVE Program's list (via the NVD API 2.0,
      with CVSS, CWE and CPE as NVD publishes them) that passes one of two tests, rebuilt every Monday since 2023-01-01:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Test A — it names a listed product.</strong> The record's CPE vendor:product, or its exact product name,
        matches one of the products in <code>config/cve_products.json</code>: agent products, agent frameworks, protocol
        implementations (MCP and A2A servers, clients and SDKs) and the inference and orchestration runtimes agents call
        through. A product is listed only if it is agentic by design; a general-purpose platform with an AI feature is not,
        because listing it would list every CVE it has.</li>
      <li><strong>Test B — the record is about agents.</strong> It carries CWE-1427 (improper neutralization of
        generative-AI prompts), or its official description uses one strong agent phrase (<em>prompt injection</em>,
        <em>MCP server</em>, <em>tool calling</em> …) or two distinct weaker ones. Every row keeps the test and the terms
        that listed it, shown on its page.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Severity, description, affected versions and references are the official record's (NVD's score first, then the
      CNA's; references NVD tags <em>Exploit</em> are dropped). The category, the attack-surface tags (from the CWE and the
      description) and the links to agent dossiers and public traces are this registry's classification. CISA's Known
      Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog is the only exploitation signal carried. A row is a candidate until a person
      includes or excludes it (its page says who did); a wrong listing is excluded with a one-line, noted override that survives every rebuild, and the record
      is never edited. No score of ours is derived from a CVE; the dossier's Security row and its CVE band show the
      registry's links to the agent (by listed product) and stop there — "no CVE linked" reads "none linked in the
      products checked", never as a finding. A search result shows the same count; an incident page lists the CVE ids
      its sources name, and a CVE page the incidents and dossiers that name it.
      Advisories without a CVE id (GitHub-only GHSAs) are not listed. The rules in full:
      <a href="https://github.com/Sushegaad/AgentDossier/blob/main/docs/cves.md" rel="noopener">docs/cves.md</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <script>
    // wide markdown tables scroll horizontally; make the scroll region reachable by keyboard (axe: scrollable-region-focusable)
    for (const t of document.querySelectorAll(".prose table")) t.setAttribute("tabindex", "0");
  </script>
</Base>
